menu "Example: Common Audio Utilities"

    config EXAMPLE_AUDIO_TX_TIMESTAMP
        bool "Synchronize multi-stream TX with timestamps"
        depends on BT_BAP_BROADCAST_SOURCE || BT_BAP_UNICAST_CLIENT
        default n
        help
            Give the SDUs of all streams of a BIG or CIG the same timestamps,
            based on the controller clock, so that the SDUs of one frame (e.g.
            the left and right audio of one sampling period) go in one event.
            Each SDU is timestamped with its SDU reference time (R0, R1, ...:
            the CIG reference point or BIG anchor point of its event plus k SDU
            intervals):

              Host                               Controller
                |  every SDU interval, one SDU (ST1): |
                |  SDU 0, 1, ... without timestamp    |
                |------------------------------------>|  on air at R0, R1..
                |  after each, LE Read ISO TX Sync    |
                |<----------------------------------->|  seq, its event's A
                |  ... until SDU j starts an event:   |
                |  Rj = A, Rn = Rj + (n - j)I         |
                |                                     |
                |  every SDU interval, one frame:     |
                |  SDU at Rn on ST1, ST2, ...         |
                |------------------------------------>|  one event for all
                |  ST3 started: joins at next frame   |
                |                                     |
                |  per SDU: handled at, its ref. time |
                |<------------------------------------|
                |  best lead of 50 SDUs < 200 us:     |
                |  one more frame at the next tick    |
                |------------------------------------>|

            The controller places SDUs without timestamps by sequence number.
            TX Sync gives only the reference point A of an event, shared by its
            SDUs, so the first stream reads it after each SDU until SDUs
            ISO interval / SDU interval - 1 apart show the same A: the first of
            them starts the event and its reference time is A. The read is a
            blocking HCI command (about 2.5 ms), so it comes after the send,
            not to delay it. One TX scheduler for the group then sends one
            frame per SDU interval, one SDU with the same timestamp on each
            stream. The best lead (how long before its reference time an SDU
            is handled) of every 50 SDUs follows the clock difference between
            the timer and the controller: below 200 us one frame is added, above
            two SDU intervals more one is skipped. A stall lowers only some
            SDUs, and the ticks then catch up, so it changes nothing; a best
            lead below minus one SDU interval is a stall or a backlog, and no
            frame is added for it. A stream started later joins at the next
            frame, so it is as early as the others. TX Sync is not read again:
            the controller places SDUs by their timestamps, on anchors of its
            own clock.

            The first 4 bytes of each SDU carry its timestamp (0 without one).
            Supported on broadcast source and unicast client with unframed
            data; used by bap/broadcast_source, bap/unicast_client,
            cap/initiator, cap/handover and tmap/central, other examples
            ignore it.

    config EXAMPLE_AUDIO_RX_ALIGN_CHECK
        bool "Check on RX that timestamped SDUs share events"
        depends on BT_BAP_BROADCAST_SINK || BT_BAP_UNICAST_SERVER
        default n
        help
            For debugging the receiving side of a BIG or CIG whose TX side uses
            "Synchronize multi-stream TX with timestamps", which puts each
            SDU's timestamp, i.e. its frame, in its first 4 bytes. SDUs of one
            SDU reference time share the RX timestamp, so they must be of one
            frame; each SDU is compared with the one before. RX timestamps up to
            100 us apart count as one: each CIS anchor is estimated on its own.

            Every 6000 compared: "RX group: total, aligned, misaligned". The
            first misaligned SDUs are logged. Each RX log also adds the valid
            (empty), error and lost SDUs of the stream.

endmenu
